Z||000003||FieroBUZZ||07-10-2001||09:58 AM||||This is one of those easy DIY projects.  Use the search function to find specific articles, but you can either chemically strip the finish or sand it off and refinish. Either way should bring back that new look.  Have fun, it's just time consuming.<BR>Gary||||reg||14
Z||000004||Mach10||07-10-2001||04:19 PM||||I know the info's there, but I'm curious:<BR>Can I just strip the clearcoat and polish every couple of days until I'm financially ready to have the wheels professionally turned and recoated? Anyone ever do this? Should I even just lay on a coat of spray-bomb clear for the time being? I kinda want to keep the gold fins. <BR>Feedback, anyone?||||reg||5
Z||000005||Leadfoot||07-10-2001||04:40 PM||||I just polished my wheels and they look fantastic.  I did not spray any clear coat since the car will not see the winter.  This site will tell you everything you need to know about stripping the wheels.<BR> <A HREF="http://members.home.net/bmcf/Fiero/Polish.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://members.home.net/bmcf/Fiero/Polish.htm</A> <P>Just a suggestion, when you strip the wheels (I used aircraft stripper available at pep boys) use a dremmel tool with a small stiff nylon brush attachment to get the hard to reach places.  Works like a charm.||||reg||1
